<article class="post-body">

<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 &#8212; The Problem</div>
<h2>Every Request Gets the Same Expensive Cluster</h2>
<p>Today's LLM inference clusters are provisioned for the worst case. A cluster serving Llama2-70B runs every request on 8 H100 GPUs at the highest GPU frequency — whether the request is a three-word greeting or a 4,000-token code review. This is the only safe default: you can't know which requests will arrive, so you keep everything ready for the hardest ones.</p>
<p>The result is enormous energy waste. A short prompt requires maybe 2 GPUs at 1.2 GHz to meet its latency target. Instead it gets 8 GPUs at 1980 MHz — over <strong>3x more energy than needed</strong>. Multiply this across millions of daily queries and an entire datacenter, and the numbers become staggering. The paper characterizes LLM inference clusters at Azure and finds serving energy could be cut by more than half without violating any SLOs.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">cluster-routing &#8212; request dispatch comparison</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to toggle</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="hm-btns">
      <button class="hm-btn active" id="prob-btn-before">Today (SinglePool)</button>
      <button class="hm-btn" id="prob-btn-after">DynamoLLM</button>
    </div>
    <canvas id="prob-canvas" style="width:100%;height:240px;display:block;border-radius:8px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div class="hm-legend" style="margin-top:.75rem">
      <div class="hm-legend-item"><div class="hm-legend-swatch" style="background:#ef4444"></div>over-provisioned (wasted energy)</div>
      <div class="hm-legend-item"><div class="hm-legend-swatch" style="background:#10b981"></div>right-sized (SLO met)</div>
      <div class="hm-legend-item"><div class="hm-legend-swatch" style="background:#f59e0b"></div>TP8 required</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Three root causes drive this heterogeneity. First, requests vary enormously in input/output length. Second, different models have vastly different parameter counts. Third, different services using the same model have different latency targets. Critically, workloads are also dynamic: the Coding service in Azure's traces shows a peak-to-valley ratio of <strong>34.6x</strong> over a week.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Insight #1:</strong> LLM workloads are highly heterogeneous in their energy-performance profiles. To achieve optimal energy under performance SLOs, different requests need to be processed separately with different configurations.
</div>
</section>

<section id="s-phases" data-code="phases">
<div class="section-no">02 &#8212; Two Phases, Different Bottlenecks</div>
<h2>Prefill Is Compute-Bound. Decode Is Memory-Bound.</h2>
<p>LLMs generate text auto-regressively, but internally they operate in two computationally distinct phases. Understanding this split is the key to understanding why different request types need different hardware configurations.</p>

<div class="phase-row">
  <div class="phase-card prefill">
    <div class="phase-title">Prefill Phase</div>
    <p class="phase-desc">All input tokens are processed in parallel in one forward pass. For a 512-token prompt, all 512 tokens flow through the model simultaneously &#8212; compute-intensive. More GPUs and higher frequency means faster prefill.</p>
    <div class="phase-stat">scales with: input length</div>
  </div>
  <div class="phase-card decode">
    <div class="phase-title">Decode Phase</div>
    <p class="phase-desc">Each output token is generated one at a time, serially &#8212; each step reads the entire KV-cache from memory. Memory-bandwidth-bound, not compute-bound. Higher frequency matters much less here.</p>
    <div class="phase-stat">scales with: output length</div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>A request processing 256 input tokens and emitting 8 output tokens spends most of its time in prefill &#8212; it benefits from high GPU frequency. A request processing 8 input tokens and emitting 500 output tokens spends almost all its time in decode &#8212; lowering frequency from 2000 MHz to 800 MHz barely increases latency but cuts power significantly.</p>

<h3>The 9-Bucket Classification</h3>
<p>DynamoLLM classifies requests into 9 buckets based on input and output length (Short/Medium/Long x Short/Medium/Long), with SLOs set to 5x the latency of an unloaded system:</p>

<table class="results-table">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Category</th><th>Input tokens</th><th>Output tokens</th><th>TTFT SLO</th><th>TBT SLO</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td><span class="mn">SS</span></td><td>&lt;256</td><td>&lt;100</td><td class="mn">250 ms</td><td class="mn">100 ms</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="mn">SM / SL</span></td><td>&lt;256</td><td>100+ / 350+</td><td class="mn">250 ms</td><td class="mn">100 ms</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="mn">MS / MM / ML</span></td><td>256&#8211;1024</td><td>various</td><td class="mn">400 ms</td><td class="mn">100 ms</td></tr>
    <tr class="highlight-row"><td><span class="mn">LS / LM / LL</span></td><td>1024&#8211;8192</td><td>various</td><td class="mn">2000 ms</td><td class="mn">100 ms</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Because LLM generation is auto-regressive, the output length is unknown at arrival &#8212; DynamoLLM uses a BERT-based proxy classifier to predict the output category.</p>
</section>

<section id="s-landscape" data-code="landscape">
<div class="section-no">03 &#8212; The Energy Landscape</div>
<h2>The Optimal Config Shifts Completely Across Request Types</h2>
<p>The core empirical finding: run Llama2-70B with medium system load (2,000 tokens/sec) and measure energy (Wh) for every combination of request type, tensor parallelism, and GPU frequency. Gray cells violate the SLO. The optimal config differs dramatically across request types.</p>

